{
  "description": "Simulated styling of the Milky Way atlas, chosen by eye. Nothing here is measured. Per style class (a catalogue layer id or a feature type): `group` = the settings switch that hides it; `color` = display colour of its guide marker, line and label; `marker` = guide symbol (none, dot, ring, cross, diamond, square); `markerPx` = smallest symbol radius in pixels; `markerFar` = camera distance (pc) beyond which the symbol is hidden; `labelFar` = camera distance (pc) beyond which the label is hidden, for rank 0, 1, 2, 3; Colours are linear light, so they are more saturated than they look on screen. `labelNear` = the label is hidden when the camera is closer than this many radii. `populations` = how a star population of the Galactic centre becomes light, per feature type: `lightPerMass` (V-band solar luminosities per solar mass: an old population for the nuclear star cluster, a young one a few million years old for the Arches and Quintuplet), `teff` (colour temperature) and `ellipticity` (flattening on the sky). A class key of the form `<file>/<type>` (for example `features-centre/disc`) styles that feature type only in that file. `standInRadiusPc` in `populations` is a simulated half-light radius used when no measured radius exists (the Arches and Quintuplet clusters). `labelAnchors` = where the label of a large structure sits, in model coordinates (kpc from Sgr A*: x away from the Sun, y towards l = 90 deg, z towards the north Galactic pole), so that labels of structures centred on Sgr A* do not pile up there. `armLabelBeta` = Galactocentric azimuth (degrees) at which each arm is labelled.",
